(17884, 129, 0, 1, 26, 'Density', '', '', 0, 0, 1, 0, '2021-03-18 23:42:14', 1, '', ''),
(17885, 129, 0, 1, 26, 'Specific heat', '', '', 0, 0, 1, 0, '2021-03-18 23:42:14', 1, '', ''),
(17886, 129, 0, 1, 15, '<p>Fluid inlet and outlet temperatures</p>', '', '', 1, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-18 23:42:14', 1, '', ''),
(17887, 129, 0, 1, 15, 'Mass flowrate (volumetric is fine if the density is known)', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-18 23:42:14', 1, '', ''),
(17888, 129, 0, 1, 15, 'Heating process efficiency', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-18 23:42:14', 1, '', ''),
(17889, 129, 0, 1, 15, 'Available floor space', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-18 23:42:14', 1, '', ''),
(17890, 129, 0, 1, 27, 'Contact thermometer (if safe)', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-18 23:42:14', 1, '', ''),
(17891, 129, 0, 1, 27, 'IR thermometer', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-18 23:42:14', 1, '', ''),
(17892, 129, 0, 1, 27, 'Ultrasonic flow meter if fluid flowrate is unknown and the temperatures are safe for the equipment', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-18 23:42:14', 1, '', ''),
(17893, 129, 0, 1, 4, 'Pressure drop across the heat exchanger could cause a loss of performance in downstream processes', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-18 23:42:14', 1, '', ''),
(17894, 129, 0, 1, 4, 'Heat exchangers can foul over time without proper maintenance and cleaning, reducing their effectiveness', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-18 23:42:14', 1, '', ''),
(17895, 129, 0, 1, 4, 'Heat exchangers can become hot and should not be placed where someone may come into contact with it inadvertently', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-18 23:42:14', 1, '', ''),
(17896, 129, 0, 1, 20, '', '/uploads/user_57/5a7446a3d0397ab25dc6ba0548b1ee14.png', 'A spiral heat exchanger is installed for heat transfer between hot sludge coming from anaerobic digestion and cool sludge coming from aerobic digestion. At this facility sludge was being heated going into the anaerobic digestor and then cooled when leaving to enter the aerobic digestor.', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-18 23:42:14', 1, '', ''),
(17897, 129, 0, 1, 20, '', '/uploads/user_57/205aebaf756291f066a0d8a016affa14.png', 'Raw milk and pasteurized milk in this process pass through a plate heat exchanger. In the pasteurization process, raw milk is heated to kill bacteria, but must be cooled afterwards. To reduce both the heating and cooling loads, the pasteurized milk preheats the raw milk while also cooling itself.', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-18 23:42:14', 1, '', ''),
(17898, 129, 0, 1, 17, 'Describes heat exchanger design for sludge in wastewater treatment facilities. This paper also contains the relevant thermodynamic properties of sludge. Understand that the characteristics of wastewater sludge is not consistent between facilities.', 'https://www.witpress.com/elibrary/wit-transactions-on-modelling-and-simulation/46/18010', 'Importance of experimental measurements and simulations for ‘sludge-to-energy’ systems', 1, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-18 23:42:14', 1, '', ''),
(17899, 129, 0, 0, 11, 'Pre-heat combustion air', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-18 23:42:14', 1, '', ''),
(17900, 129, 0, 1, 10, 'Look to see if a high temperature oven/furnace is currently recycling hot air from stack.', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-18 23:42:14', 1, '', ''),
(17901, 129, 0, 1, 8, 'Two different heat exchangers can be used for this opportunity, recuperators and regenerators. ', '%zXz%', '%zXz%', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-18 23:42:14', 1, '', ''),
(17902, 129, 0, 2, 31, '<p><strong>Recuperators </strong>work like a standard air-to-air heat exchanger. There is a hot flow and a cold flow in which energy is transferred between with the use of internal tubes or plates. The two streams are kept in separate ducts to keep the gasses from mixing.</p>', '%zXz%', '%zXz%', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-18 23:42:14', 1, '', ''),
(17903, 129, 0, 2, 1, 'Design versatility makes it easier to apply a recuperator to most applications. ', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-18 23:42:14', 1, '', ''),
(17904, 129, 0, 2, 2, 'Temperature applications are dependent on material available for heat exchanger tubes. ', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-18 23:42:14', 1, '', ''),
(17905, 129, 0, 2, 20, '', 'https://cdn4.explainthatstuff.com/how-heat-exchangers-work.png', 'Recuperator Schematic', 0, 0, 0, 26, '2021-03-18 23:42:14', 1, '', 'sha384-ponrRYl2z9C4TBbae/fGuKY3dHmCvax5GBs1jghINbuFVkfScE/8Ryk3EoasS5c+'),
(17906, 129, 0, 2, 31, '<p><strong>Regenerators </strong>act as heating vessels for both the combustion air and flue gases. Regenerators alternate between heating the storage medium and then taking heat from it. Usually at least two regenerators and burners are needed so that the process is uninterrupted.</p>', '%zXz%', '%zXz%', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-18 23:42:14', 1, '', ''),
(17907, 129, 0, 2, 1, 'Ceramic heat sinks allow for applications in extreme temperature environments.', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-18 23:42:14', 1, '', ''),
(17908, 129, 0, 2, 1, 'Resistant to corrosive environments. ', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-18 23:42:14', 1, '', ''),
(17909, 129, 0, 2, 2, 'Large amount of space required.', '', '', 1, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-18 23:42:14', 1, '', ''),
(17910, 129, 0, 2, 20, '', 'https://image.slidesharecdn.com/heatexchangertypesandapplication-171015153006/95/heat-exchanger-types-and-application-13-638.jpg?cb=1508081873', 'Regenerator Schematic', 0, 0, 0, 27, '2021-03-18 23:42:14', 1, '', ''),
(17911, 129, 0, 1, 4, '<p>Take care not to reduce exhaust stack temperature too low as it can cause corrosion. This happens when the surface temperature of the stack is lower than the dew point of the flue gas flowing through the stack, causing moisture to condense and accumulate.</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-18 23:42:14', 1, '', ''),
(17912, 129, 0, 1, 8, 'Some flue gasses are corrosive and can damage installed heat exchanges. When performing an analysis, research which heat exchangers are best designed for the process in question. ', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-18 23:42:14', 1, '', ''),
(17913, 129, 0, 1, 15, 'Flue gas temperature', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-18 23:42:14', 1, '', ''),
(17914, 129, 0, 1, 15, 'Inlet air temperature', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-18 23:42:14', 1, '', ''),
(17915, 129, 0, 1, 15, 'Fuel feed rate', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-18 23:42:14', 1, '', ''),
(17916, 129, 0, 1, 15, 'Excess oxygen', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-18 23:42:14', 1, '', ''),
(17917, 129, 0, 1, 15, 'Operation hours', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-18 23:42:14', 1, '', ''),
(17918, 129, 0, 1, 27, 'Combustion analyzer', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-18 23:42:14', 1, '', ''),
(17919, 129, 0, 1, 17, '', 'https://www.energy.gov/sites/prod/files/2014/05/f16/et_preheated.pdf', 'D.O.E. Tip Sheet', 1, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-18 23:42:14', 1, '', 'sha384-TblbGC+/tZjmTy+XOd9DxzyzgO3No9bE5OTzqlv3nPnlazmkomeq2nfUXhd4jHQm'),
(17920, 129, 0, 1, 17, '', 'https://www.energysolutionscenter.org/gas_solutions/regenerators_and_recuperators.aspx#:~:text=Regenerators%20and%20recuperators%20are%20heat,metallic%20heat%20exchanger%20(recuperators).&text=The%20heat%20recovered%20by%20a,combustion%20air%20to%20a%20furnace.', 'Regenerators vs. Recuperators ', 1, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-18 23:42:14', 1, '', ''),
(18045, 105, 0, 0, 15, 'Condensate flow and temperature', '', '', 1, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-19 00:27:16', 1, '', ''),
(18046, 105, 0, 0, 11, 'Return more/all condensate back to the boiler', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-19 00:27:16', 1, '', ''),
(18047, 105, 0, 0, 11, 'Recover Flash Steam', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-19 00:27:16', 1, '', ''),
(18048, 105, 0, 1, 31, '<p>Typically a percentage of condensate flashes back to steam when it enters an atmospheric condensate line from a high pressure steam line. A low pressure condensate line can be used to retain and direct this flash steam. It can be combined with cold incoming makeup water in a flash tank capturing the \"distilled water\" of the flash steam and increasing the final makeup water temperature. Often there is more energy available in the flash steam than can be used with the makeup water. It can also be used in other low temperature processes that can use the moisture and heat. (An example might be low pressure steam used for block conditioning of log sections before peeling into a veneer.)</p>', '', '', 1, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-19 00:27:16', 1, '', ''),
(18049, 105, 0, 1, 10, 'Steam being released into atmosphere from the boiler system', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-19 00:27:16', 1, '', ''),
(18050, 105, 0, 1, 1, 'Payback period is usually within a year', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-19 00:27:16', 1, '', ''),
(18051, 105, 0, 1, 1, 'Capturing and reusing the steam can reduce hog fuel by 14.5%', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-19 00:27:16', 1, '', ''),
(18052, 105, 0, 1, 1, 'Condensate does not require any chemical treatment other than condensate polishing. ', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-19 00:27:16', 1, '', ''),
(18053, 105, 0, 2, 31, 'A condensate polisher is similar to a water softener. Polishing removes the trace amount of mineral that are dissolved in the condensate after running though the boiler. ', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-19 00:27:16', 1, '', ''),
(18054, 105, 0, 1, 2, 'Maintenance crews will need training to work with steam recovery systems', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-19 00:27:16', 1, '', ''),
(18055, 105, 0, 1, 2, 'There are usually no incentives for this opportunity, because the system usually pays for itself within a year.', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-19 00:27:16', 1, '', ''),
(18056, 105, 0, 1, 3, 'A good recovery system can collect up to 80% of the condensate', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-19 00:27:16', 1, '', ''),
(18057, 105, 0, 1, 3, 'Steam recovery is most efficient when waste heat is high and flow is continuous', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-19 00:27:16', 1, '', ''),
(18058, 105, 0, 1, 8, 'Multiple different types of steam traps available to fit company needs: Mechanical, Thermodynamic, or Thermostatic', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-19 00:27:16', 1, '', ''),
(18059, 105, 0, 1, 17, 'Contains specific information about flash steam recovery', 'https://www.energy.gov/sites/prod/files/2014/05/f16/steam12_lowpressure_steam.pdf', 'DOE Tip Sheet Flash Steam Recovery', 1, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-19 00:27:16', 1, '', 'sha384-jD3QcNeDY/uVNOe2UN6icM4RNWzS1i98YvRLWJYxUarqKlmPERcCJmX1fgFL9Gws'),
(18060, 105, 0, 1, 21, '', 'https://oregonstate.app.box.com/file/337450150435', 'Flash Steam Recovery', 0, 1, 0, 0, '2021-03-22 20:57:58', 1, '', ''),
(18061, 105, 0, 0, 11, 'Fix or replace faulty steam traps', '', '', 1, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-19 00:27:16', 1, '', ''),
(18062, 105, 0, 1, 8, 'Use an ultrasonic testing equipment to detect faulty steam traps', '', '', 1, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-19 00:27:16', 1, '', ''),
(18063, 105, 0, 0, 23, 'Explains how steam traps work, brief history and lists several different types of traps in detail.', 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IiRyxcCBTa0', 'Let\'s Talk Steam Traps', 1, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-19 00:27:16', 1, '', ''),
(18064, 105, 0, 0, 24, 'Contains several pages with useful calculations for steam systems. This link leads to the flash steam page.', 'https://www.tlv.com/global/US/steam-theory/introduction-to-condensate-recovery.html', 'TLV Steam Theory', 1, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-19 00:27:16', 1, '', ''),
(18251, 260, 0, 0, 31, '<p>System lift is the difference between suction and discharge pressure at the compressor stage in the refrigeration cycle. Lift can be reduced by either increasing the suction pressure, decreasing the discharge pressure, or a combination of both. Minimizing lift improves system efficiency by increasing efficiency at the compressor stage.</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-21 18:02:03', 1, '', ''),
(18252, 260, 0, 0, 11, '<p>Increase suction pressure (raise evaporator temperature)</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-21 18:02:03', 1, '', ''),
(18253, 260, 0, 1, 31, '<p>Increasing suction pressure raises compressor capacity, thereby allowing the compressor to operate at a lower percentage of its full-load capacity.</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-21 18:02:03', 1, '', ''),
(18254, 260, 0, 1, 10, '<p>Suction temperature is lower than 10 to 15°F below the target space/product temperature</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-21 18:02:03', 1, '', ''),
(18255, 260, 0, 2, 12, '<p>Add evaporator capacity to increase suction pressure. Compressor savings will generally pay for the cost of installing and operating additional evaporators.</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-21 18:02:03', 1, '', ''),
(18256, 260, 0, 1, 10, '<p>Back-pressure regulation is used to raise pressure to selected system branches with higher temperature loads</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-21 18:02:03', 1, '', ''),
(18257, 260, 0, 2, 12, '<p>Operate loads requiring higher suction pressures and loads requiring lower suction pressures on separate systems</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-21 18:02:03', 1, '', ''),
(18258, 260, 0, 1, 7, '<p>Set suction pressure to put the evaporator fan power and compressor power at a combined minimum. If fan savings are unavailable the suction pressure should be set as high as possible.</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 21, '2021-03-21 18:02:03', 1, '', ''),
(18259, 260, 0, 1, 8, '<p>Increasing suction pressure may allow operators to turn off a compressor or to use a smaller compressor in place of a larger one.</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 21, '2021-03-21 18:02:03', 1, '', ''),
(18260, 260, 0, 1, 8, '<p>Install larger evaporator coils to maintain cooling capacity while decreasing the approach temperature difference</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 21, '2021-03-21 18:02:03', 1, '', ''),
(18261, 260, 0, 1, 3, '<p>2-3% compressor power reduction per 1°F increase in suction temperature for centrifugal machines</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 21, '2021-03-21 18:02:03', 1, '', ''),
(18262, 260, 0, 1, 15, '<p>Current and proposed suction pressures/temperatures</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-21 18:02:03', 1, '', ''),
(18263, 260, 0, 1, 15, '<p>Current compressor energy use</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-21 18:02:03', 1, '', ''),
(18264, 260, 0, 2, 12, '<p>Set data loggers to record power consumption over the compressor(s) typical operating range.</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-21 18:02:03', 1, '', ''),
(18265, 260, 0, 1, 20, '', '/uploads/user_58/697b47f5ecb8474a9482471ddce67959.png', '<p>Pressure Enthalpy Diagram - Effect of Increasing Suction Pressure</p>', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-21 18:02:03', 1, '', ''),
(18266, 260, 0, 1, 31, '<p>The transition from point 4 to point 1 on the enthalpy pressure diagram represents the pressure and enthalpy changes associated with compression. Increasing suction pressure reduces both the pressure change (lift) and enthalpy change (work).</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-21 18:02:03', 1, '', ''),
(18267, 260, 0, 1, 4, '<p>Energy savings may only be available if efficient part-load compressor controls are used.</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-21 18:02:03', 1, '', ''),
(18268, 260, 0, 0, 11, '<p>Decrease discharge/condensing pressure</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-21 18:02:03', 1, '', ''),
(18269, 260, 0, 1, 10, '<p>The condensers are operating below their full capacity.</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-21 18:02:03', 1, '', ''),
(18270, 260, 0, 1, 10, '<p>Approach temperature in the condensers exceeds 20°F</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-21 18:02:03', 1, '', ''),
(18271, 260, 0, 2, 12, '<p>Add condensing capacity to lower the approach temperature to 10-15°F above ambient. Compressor savings typically pay for the cost of installing and operating additional compressors.</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-21 18:02:03', 1, '', ''),
(18272, 260, 0, 1, 7, '<p>Set a minimum discharge temperature, above which the discharge will be allowed to \"float\", responding to changes in the ambient saturation temperature.</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-21 18:02:03', 1, '', ''),
(18273, 260, 0, 1, 8, '<p>Keep an eye out for gradually increasing discharge temperature. This may indicate scaling in the condenser tubes which reduces heat transfer. Implement adequate water treatment to avoid this potential issue.</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-21 18:02:03', 1, '', ''),
(18274, 260, 0, 1, 3, '<p>The refrigerant condensing temperature should be at least 35°F above the refrigerant evaporator temperature.</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 20, '2021-03-21 18:02:03', 1, '', ''),
(18275, 260, 0, 1, 3, '<p>1-1.5% compressor power reduction per 1°F decrease in condensing temperature</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 20, '2021-03-21 18:02:03', 1, '', ''),
(18276, 260, 0, 1, 15, '<p>Current and proposed discharge pressures</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-21 18:02:03', 1, '', ''),
(18277, 260, 0, 1, 15, '<p>Dry and wet bulb discharge temperatures: current and proposed</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-21 18:02:03', 1, '', ''),
(18278, 260, 0, 1, 15, '<p>Current compressor energy use</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-21 18:02:03', 1, '', ''),
(18279, 260, 0, 2, 12, '<p>Set data loggers to record power consumption over the compressor(s) typical operating range.</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-21 18:02:03', 1, '', ''),
(18280, 260, 0, 1, 4, '<p>Setting condensing pressure too low may cause expansion valves to malfunction. If so consider replacing the expansion valve to allow lowering discharge pressure.</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-21 18:02:03', 1, '', ''),
(18281, 260, 0, 1, 4, '<p>Condensing temperature may be limited by the use of liquid-injection oil cooling. If so consider switching to thermo-syphon oil cooling to allow lowering discharge pressure (and gain other energy savings).</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-21 18:02:03', 1, '', ''),
(18282, 260, 0, 2, 33, '<p></p>', '#card-267', 'See thermo-syphon cooling ', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-05-26 20:25:43', 1, '', ''),
(18283, 260, 0, 1, 4, '<p>A higher discharge pressure may be required to drive refrigerant to to some end of line applications. If so consider lowering discharge pressure and adding a liquid pump to deliver the refrigerant.</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-21 18:02:03', 1, '', ''),
(18284, 260, 0, 1, 20, '', '/uploads/user_58/9875ac7b565991bba7a34c7c470c3c58.png', '<p>Pressure Enthalpy Diagram - Effect of Decreasing Discharge Pressure</p>', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-21 18:02:03', 1, '', ''),
(18285, 260, 0, 1, 31, '<p>The transition from point 4 to point 1 on the enthalpy pressure diagram represents the pressure and enthalpy changes associated with compression. Decreasing discharge pressure reduces both the pressure change (lift) and enthalpy change (work).</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-21 18:02:03', 1, '', ''),
(18286, 260, 0, 0, 11, '<p>Implement floating discharge pressure</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-21 18:02:03', 1, '', ''),
(18287, 260, 0, 1, 31, '<p>Typically discharge pressure is controlled with a combination of condenser fan controls and a pressure valve located between the condensers and expansion device. With floating head controls this valve is disconnected to allow pressure to automatically follow the outside air temperature, leading to year-round energy savings. To avoid pressure dropping too low a liquid pump is placed after the condensers. </p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-21 18:02:03', 1, '', ''),
(18288, 260, 0, 1, 1, '<p>Eliminates the need to manually change pressure settings throughout the year</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-21 18:02:03', 1, '', ''),
(18289, 260, 0, 1, 1, '<p>Reduces compressor power and runtime</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-21 18:02:03', 1, '', ''),
(18290, 260, 0, 1, 1, '<p>Cooler liquid refrigerant has more cooling capacity, which translates to greater thermodynamic efficiency and volumetric compressing efficiency</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-21 18:02:03', 1, '', ''),
(18291, 260, 0, 1, 8, '<p>Recent weather data is available from the National Weather Services\' website, weather.gov</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-21 18:02:03', 1, '', ''),
(18382, 305, 0, 0, 22, '<p>This slideshow for the Better Plants Program serves as a primer for the importance of energy efficiency projects for water/wastewater plants.</p>', 'https://betterbuildingssolutioncenter.energy.gov/sites/default/files/Primer%20on%20energy%20efficiency%20in%20water%20and%20wastewater%20plants_0.pdf', 'Primer on Energy Efficiency for Water and Wastewater Treatment', 1, 0, 0, 0, '2021-04-13 16:39:20', 0, '', ''),
(18383, 305, 0, 0, 24, '<p>The two resources below come from this site.</p>', 'https://iac.university/webinars', 'IAC University Webinars Page including \"IC 2020 Student Research Award Winner - Framework for Net-Zero Wastewater Treatment Plan\"', 1, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-22 23:32:17', 0, '', ''),
(18384, 305, 0, 0, 23, '<p>IC 2020 Student Research Award Winner - Framework for Net-Zero Wastewater Treatment Plan. A YouTube video of an IAC.University Webinar on March 3, 2021.</p>', 'https://youtu.be/noPvetdC0Y0', 'Framework for Net-Zero Wastewater Treatment Plan', 1, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-22 23:32:17', 0, '', ''),
(18385, 305, 0, 0, 22, '<p>Downloadable PDF of slideshow used in the above YouTube video of an IAC.University Webinar: Framework for Net-Zero Wastewater Treatment Plan</p>', 'https://iac.university/file/general/CHP%20TAP%20Training%20for%20IACs%20-%20February%2011%202021.pdf', 'Framework for Net-Zero Wastewater Treatment Plan', 1, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-22 23:32:17', 0, '', 'sha384-9jflPauZYdBmNsw2dSMtkfJfnX1h4lArBFCRou+iHYifuBnmadGWE79eCjOLBurb'),
(18386, 305, 0, 0, 22, '<p>Links to webinars, presentations, and other information regarding energy efficiency in municipal water treatment facilities.</p>', 'https://betterbuildingssolutioncenter.energy.gov/better-plants/special-initiatives/water-and-wastewater', 'U.S. DOE Better Buildings - Water and Wastewater', 1, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-22 23:32:17', 0, '', ''),
(18387, 107, 0, 0, 11, 'Replace standard efficiency motors with NEMA premium efficiency motors', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-23 20:09:58', 1, '', ''),
(18388, 107, 0, 1, 10, 'Standard efficiency motors used in high energy consumer applications ', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-23 20:09:58', 1, '', ''),
(18389, 107, 0, 1, 8, '<p>It may not be economical to replace the motor immediately. Instead, mark the motor so it is clear that it should be replaced with a premium efficiency motor.</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-23 20:09:58', 1, '', ''),
(18390, 107, 0, 1, 8, '<p>Estimate 3.5 to 5 percent increase in efficiency for 5 to 30 HP, 3 to 3.5 percent for 40 to 125 HP motors, and 2 percent for motors 150 HP and above.</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-23 20:09:58', 1, '', ''),
(18391, 107, 0, 0, 11, 'Replace oversized motors', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-23 20:09:58', 1, '', ''),
(18392, 107, 0, 1, 31, 'It is common for a facility to choose an oversized motor as a conservative measure. Motors consume the least amount of energy when they operate at their highest efficiency. For most motors, this is from 75% to 110% of their rated load. As motor loading drops below 50%, the efficiency and power factor drops rapidly. ', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-23 20:09:58', 1, '', ''),
(18393, 107, 0, 1, 10, '<p>Motor consistently operates at less than half of full-load</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-23 20:09:58', 1, '', ''),
(18394, 107, 0, 1, 15, 'Power measurements (kW) to determine motor loading is preferred over amperage reading because they take into account the changes in power factor and amperage that occur as the motor loading changes', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-23 20:09:58', 1, '', ''),
(18395, 107, 0, 0, 11, '<p>Replace standard V-belts with notched V-belts</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-23 20:09:58', 1, '', ''),
(18396, 107, 0, 1, 31, '<p>Notched V-belts have grooves perpendicular to the length of the belt to reduce bending resistance</p>', '%zXz%', '%zXz%', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-23 20:09:58', 1, '', ''),
(18397, 107, 0, 1, 10, '<p>Numerous belt-driven motors are used throughout a facility</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-23 20:09:58', 1, '', ''),
(18398, 107, 0, 1, 3, '<p>Notched V-belts are approximately 2% more efficient than standard V-belts. The OSU EEC uses 1.5% as a conservative estimate.</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-23 20:09:58', 1, '', ''),
(18399, 107, 0, 1, 14, 'Are V-belts or synchronous belts more suited to the application?', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-23 20:09:58', 1, '', ''),
(18400, 107, 0, 1, 1, '<p>Notched V-belts run cooler, last longer, and are more efficient than standard V-belts</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-23 20:09:58', 1, '', ''),
(18401, 107, 0, 1, 1, '<p>Notched V-belts do not require retrofits if standard V-belts are already used</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-23 20:09:58', 1, '', ''),
(18402, 107, 0, 1, 1, '<p>Notched V-belts are more suitable than synchronous drives if vibrational damping is needed, or the application causes sudden torque changes</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-23 20:09:58', 1, '', ''),
(18403, 107, 0, 1, 2, '<p>Sharp efficiency reduction at high torque due to increased slippage</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-23 20:09:58', 1, '', ''),
(18404, 107, 0, 1, 2, '<p>Like standard V-belts, notched V-belts degrade in efficiency over time if not properly maintained</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-23 20:09:58', 1, '', ''),
(18405, 107, 0, 1, 2, 'V-belts may perform worse in dirty environments than synchronous belts', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-23 20:09:58', 1, '', ''),
(18406, 107, 0, 1, 12, '<p>Install notched V-belts as old standard V-belts wear out</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-23 20:09:58', 1, '', ''),
(18407, 107, 0, 1, 7, 'Regular scheduled maintenance and re-tensioning ', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-23 20:09:58', 1, '', ''),
(18408, 107, 0, 1, 20, '', '/uploads/user_52/23dd50688eb5cdbe04bfec59a02df28c.png', '<p>Thermal image of notched vs. standard V-belt</p>', 0, 1, 0, 28, '2021-03-23 20:09:58', 1, '', ''),
(18409, 107, 0, 1, 21, 'An OSU EEC Analysis Template in Microsoft Excel Format', 'https://drive.google.com/file/d/19tm7mcwn9jYIj_xDkJ7Ki9buPw0gc2Kl/view?usp=sharing', 'Install Notched V-Belts Template', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-23 20:09:58', 1, '', ''),
(18410, 107, 0, 0, 11, '<p>Replace V-Belt drives with synchronous belt drives (sometimes called high-torque drives)</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-23 20:09:58', 1, '', ''),
(18411, 107, 0, 1, 31, 'Synchronous drives use toothed belts and mated grooved sprockets to transfer power based on positive engagement rather than friction. Synchronous belt drives operate more efficiently and require less maintenance than V-belt drives.', '%zXz%', '%zXz%', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-23 20:09:58', 1, '', ''),
(18412, 107, 0, 1, 10, 'Numerous belt-driven motors are used throughout a facility ', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-23 20:09:58', 1, '', ''),
(18413, 107, 0, 1, 3, 'Synchronous drives consistently operate with 98% efficiency', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-23 20:09:58', 1, '', ''),
(18414, 107, 0, 1, 14, 'Are V-belts or synchronous belts more suited to the application?', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-23 20:09:58', 1, '', ''),
(18415, 107, 0, 1, 1, 'Maintain efficiency over a wide load range', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-23 20:09:58', 1, '', ''),
(18416, 107, 0, 1, 1, 'Work well in oily and wet environments', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-23 20:09:58', 1, '', ''),
(18417, 107, 0, 1, 1, 'Require minimal maintenance and re-tensioning ', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-23 20:09:58', 1, '', ''),
(18418, 107, 0, 1, 2, 'Require installation of mating grooved sprockets', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-23 20:09:58', 1, '', ''),
(18419, 107, 0, 1, 2, 'Noisier and transfer more vibration than V-belts', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-23 20:09:58', 1, '', ''),
(18420, 107, 0, 1, 2, 'Vulnerable to sudden torque changes that can shear the belt\'s teeth', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-23 20:09:58', 1, '', ''),
(18421, 107, 0, 1, 4, '<p>If the belt is meant to be the weakest link in the motor driven system, for example, if the system tends to jam, then a standard V-belt is the better choice. A synchronous belt is less likely to be the failure point in a jam, leading to potential damage of more expensive components.</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-23 20:09:58', 1, '', ''),
(18422, 107, 0, 1, 12, 'Consider consulting a power transmission specialist to determine viability and savings potential from retrofitting V-belt drives with synchronous belts. Install notched belts where synchronous are not cost effective.', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-23 20:09:58', 1, '', ''),
(18426, 119, 0, 0, 10, 'Equipment is idle for significant periods of time', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-23 20:16:18', 1, '', ''),
(18427, 119, 0, 0, 11, 'Manually reduce equipment operation time', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-23 20:16:18', 1, '', ''),
(18428, 119, 0, 1, 31, '<p>Turn off equipment during lunch, breaks, and other times when equipment is not in use.</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-23 20:16:18', 1, '', ''),
(18429, 119, 0, 1, 1, 'No cost option', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-23 20:16:18', 1, '', ''),
(18430, 119, 0, 1, 2, 'This measure is only as reliable as the operators', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-23 20:16:18', 1, '', ''),
(18431, 119, 0, 0, 11, 'Operate equipment in batches rather than continuously ', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-23 20:16:18', 1, '', ''),
(18432, 119, 0, 1, 31, '<p>A piece of equipment like a grinder may run continously although material only runs through it occasionally. An alternative approach is to let material collect and assign someone to periodically turn it on to process the material in batches.</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-23 20:16:18', 1, '', ''),
(18433, 119, 0, 1, 1, 'No cost option', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-23 20:16:18', 1, '', ''),
(18434, 119, 0, 1, 2, 'Batch processing also has potential for increasing demand charges if the equipment is more heavily loaded.', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-23 20:16:18', 1, '', ''),
(18435, 119, 0, 0, 11, 'Interlock equipment with a related process', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-23 20:16:18', 1, '', ''),
(18436, 119, 0, 1, 31, '<p>If a particular piece of equipment is dedicated to specific process that requires additional equipment, they can all be interlocked so all will be de-energized when the operator turns off one piece of equipment.</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-23 20:16:18', 1, '', ''),
(18437, 119, 0, 1, 1, 'More reliable then having operators turn of multiple pieces of equipment', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-23 20:16:18', 1, '', ''),
(18438, 119, 0, 0, 11, 'Automatically control equipment operation time', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-23 20:16:18', 1, '', ''),
(18439, 119, 0, 1, 31, '<p>Timers, level sensors, material sensors, and other controls can be used to automatically turn off equipment that is not currently being utilized and automatically turn on when needed.</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-23 20:16:18', 1, '', ''),
(18440, 119, 0, 1, 1, 'Can obtain efficiencies of batch processing automatically', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-23 20:16:18', 1, '', ''),
(18441, 119, 0, 1, 4, 'Special care must be taken to avoid creating a safety hazard', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-23 20:16:18', 1, '', ''),
(18442, 119, 0, 0, 11, '<p>Consider load shedding</p>', '', '', 0, 1, 0, 0, '2021-03-23 20:16:18', 1, '', ''),
(18463, 118, 0, 0, 11, 'Use variable frequency drives where appropriate', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-23 20:53:58', 1, '', ''),
(18464, 118, 0, 1, 10, '<p>Large motors attached to loads that are resistance controlled (throttle valves, brakes, etc.)</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-23 20:53:58', 1, '', ''),
(18465, 118, 0, 1, 4, '<p>Installing variable frequency drives may induce harmonics into the electrical distribution system. A system with many capacitors installed can be particularly vulnerable unless \"tuned\" by a professional. Some installations are relatively insensitive while others are very sensitive to this problem.</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-23 20:53:58', 1, '', ''),
(18472, 9, 0, 0, 1, 'Versatile. Offers compact energy density. ', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-29 15:57:47', 1, '', ''),
(18473, 9, 0, 0, 1, '<p>Spark free for potentially explosive environments.</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-29 15:57:47', 1, '', ''),
(18474, 9, 0, 0, 1, '<p>Can be used as an easy quick fix for many issues.</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-29 15:57:47', 1, '', ''),
(18475, 9, 0, 0, 1, '<p>Familiar utility for industrial personnel.</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-29 15:57:47', 1, '', ''),
(18476, 9, 0, 0, 1, 'A single mechanical energy input at the compressor can be distributed throughout a facility. ', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-29 15:57:47', 1, '', ''),
(18477, 16, 0, 0, 4, '<p>Take care to avoid potential dangerous air injection associated with directing compressed air flow directly onto skin.</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-29 15:58:04', 1, '', ''),
(18478, 18, 0, 0, 3, '<p>Expect a 1% drop in compressor energy and cost per 2 PSI in compressor outlet pressure drop.</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-29 16:00:01', 1, '', ''),
(18479, 18, 0, 0, 3, '<p>85 psi is the standard required minimum inlet pressure for most common industrial pneumatic equipment.</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-29 16:00:01', 1, '', ''),
(18480, 18, 0, 0, 3, '<p>80% to 90% of energy for compressed air is lost as heat.</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-29 16:00:01', 1, '', ''),
(18481, 18, 0, 0, 3, '<p>Over 5 HP of electrical power is required for each 1 HP of compressed air power.</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-29 16:00:01', 1, '', ''),
(18485, 19, 0, 0, 8, '<p>Use a pressure gage with standard quick connects typically used in compressed air lines to diagnose line pressure drops.</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-29 16:01:01', 1, '', ''),
(18486, 19, 0, 0, 8, '<p>Determine the leak load by checking compressor output when there is no productive air use.</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-29 16:01:01', 1, '', ''),
(19317, 89, 0, 0, 2, '<p>Energy and mass (steam &amp; condensate) losses in the distribution system can result in significant overall system efficiency reductions.</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-31 21:33:41', 1, '', ''),
(19318, 89, 0, 0, 2, '<p>Regular maintenance is required to maintain boiler efficiency.</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-31 21:33:41', 1, '', ''),
(19327, 208, 0, 0, 31, 'It is always important to cite where information is coming from. A library of sources exists on each page that referenced within content-carrying cards. ', '%zXz%', '%zXz%', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-31 22:40:52', 0, '', ''),
(19328, 208, 0, 1, 26, 'To add a reference, click \"Add Source\" in the header of any page in Edit Mode. ', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-31 22:40:52', 0, '', ''),
(19329, 208, 0, 1, 26, 'Use IEEE formatting for all references and include a URL if applicable. ', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-31 22:40:52', 0, '', ''),
(19330, 208, 0, 1, 26, 'To connect a source to an item, click \"Edit Card\" and use the source dropdown menu to the right of the item to select the source. ', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-31 22:40:52', 0, '', ''),
(19331, 208, 0, 1, 17, '', 'https://oregonstate.box.com/s/rm1jtvnmfhb2oyt1wyvzaztgc3w1ww5q', 'IEEE Reference Guide', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-05-24 02:54:24', 0, '', ''),
(19332, 208, 0, 0, 31, '<p>It may be advantageous to reference a page within the Walkthrough Checklist website. This could be used to reference another technology page or another recommendation that keys into the one you are writing. To do this, follow the steps below.</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-31 22:40:52', 0, '', ''),
(19333, 208, 0, 1, 26, '<p>Choose the card you want to reference and select \"Review Card\"</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-31 22:40:52', 0, '', ''),
(19334, 208, 0, 1, 26, '<p>Copy the card number. This will be referenced as \"Recommendation Name (#card-10).\" In this case, copy the \"(#card-10).\"</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-31 22:40:52', 0, '', ''),
(19335, 208, 0, 1, 26, '<p>Go back to the card you want the reference to appear in and select \"Add Site Resource\" at the bottom of the edit page.</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-31 22:40:52', 0, '', ''),
(19336, 208, 0, 1, 26, '<p>Select the \"Bookmark\" option as the icon. Paste the card number from above and past it in the URL. Choose an appropriate name for the link.</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-03-31 22:40:52', 0, '', ''),
(19337, 268, 0, 0, 11, 'Install VFDs on condenser fans', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-04-05 20:23:28', 1, '', ''),
(19338, 268, 0, 1, 31, '<p>Condenser fans often cycle on and off to maintain condensing temperature resulting in an average part-load power. A variable frequency drive can reduce this part-load power by efficiently slowing fan speed, taking advantage of cube law power savings.</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-04-05 20:23:28', 1, '', ''),
(19339, 268, 0, 1, 10, 'Single or two-speed fans demand a lot of power', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-04-05 20:23:28', 1, '', ''),
(19340, 268, 0, 1, 3, 'Ideal fan power is proportional to the cube of fan speed with a VFD', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-04-05 20:23:28', 1, '', ''),
(19341, 268, 0, 1, 3, 'At 50% speed fans will consume about 15% of full-load power while still providing 50% airflow', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-04-05 20:23:28', 1, '', ''),
(19342, 268, 0, 1, 33, '<p>Use the FCAT, an OSU EEC Microsoft Excel analysis tool to estimate fan energy savings</p>', '#card-142', 'Analysis Tools', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-05-26 21:10:07', 1, '', ''),
(19343, 268, 0, 0, 15, '<p>Data to collect</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-04-05 20:23:28', 1, '', ''),
(19344, 268, 0, 1, 26, 'Minimum condensing temperature', '', '', 0, 0, 1, 0, '2021-04-05 20:23:28', 1, '', ''),
(19345, 268, 0, 1, 26, 'Total fan power', '', '', 0, 0, 1, 0, '2021-04-05 20:23:28', 1, '', ''),
(19346, 268, 0, 1, 26, 'Wet and dry bulb temperatures (use bin weather data if temperature cannot be recorded over time)', '', '', 0, 0, 1, 0, '2021-04-05 20:23:28', 1, '', ''),
(19347, 268, 0, 1, 26, 'Condenser MATD (measured with all fans turned on)', '', '', 0, 0, 1, 0, '2021-04-05 20:23:28', 1, '', ''),
(19348, 268, 0, 1, 26, 'Fan use factor (may be available from trend logs on electronic control systems)', '', '', 0, 0, 1, 0, '2021-04-05 20:23:28', 1, '', ''),
(19349, 268, 0, 0, 11, 'Install VFDs on evaporator fans', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-04-05 20:23:28', 1, '', ''),
(19350, 268, 0, 1, 31, '<p>Typically evaporator fans cycle on and off to maintain product or storage space temperature, resulting in an average part-load power. A variable frequency drive can reduce this part-load power by efficiently slowing fan speed.</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-04-05 20:23:28', 1, '', ''),
(19351, 268, 0, 1, 10, 'Single or two-speed fans demand a lot of power', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-04-05 20:23:28', 1, '', ''),
(19352, 268, 0, 1, 1, 'Improved product quality through maintaining consistent temperature control and constant (although lower) airflow', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 25, '2021-04-05 20:23:28', 1, '', ''),
(19353, 268, 0, 1, 1, 'Improved working conditions through reduced wind-chill and fan noise ', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 25, '2021-04-05 20:23:28', 1, '', ''),
(19354, 268, 0, 1, 1, 'Ability to adjust fan speed to meet specific demands of different products', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 25, '2021-04-05 20:23:28', 1, '', ''),
(19355, 268, 0, 1, 2, 'Requires higher capital investment that may result in long paybacks for seasonal storage applications', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-04-05 20:23:28', 1, '', ''),
(19356, 268, 0, 1, 3, 'Ideal fan power is proportional to the cube of fan speed with a VFD', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-04-05 20:23:28', 1, '', ''),
(19357, 268, 0, 1, 3, 'At 50% speed fans will consume about 15% of full-load power while still providing 50% airflow', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-04-05 20:23:28', 1, '', ''),
(19358, 268, 0, 1, 33, '<p>Use the FCAT, an OSU EEC Microsoft Excel analysis tool to estimate fan energy savings</p>', '#card-142', 'Analysis Tools', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-04-05 20:23:28', 1, '', ''),
(19359, 268, 0, 0, 15, '<p>Data to collect</p>', '', '', 0, 0, 0, 0, '2021-04-05 20:23:28', 1, '', ''),
(19360, 268, 0, 1, 26, 'Suction pressure', '', '', 0, 0, 1, 0, '2021-04-05 20:23:28', 1, '', ''),
(19361, 268, 0, 1, 26, 'Total fan power', '', '', 0, 0, 1, 0, '2021-04-05 20:23:28', 1, '', ''),
(19362, 268, 0, 1, 26, 'Dry bulb temperature', '', '', 0, 0, 1, 0, '2021-04-05 20:23:28', 1, '', ''),
